{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01WjPaptHTPza7nnc2dhkiCY","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context carefully and propose tasks for release 00.0.\n\n## Project Summary\n\n**Problem**: Classic Unix utilities lack formal specifications. Re-implementations diverge in observable behavior. This project derives formal specs from C source code, generates Go implementations using AI, and verifies parity through differential testing.\n\n**Architecture**: Four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify). Key components:\n- `cmd/` - Per-utility Go implementations\n- `pkg/sys/` - Darwin/Linux syscall abstractions\n- `pkg/format/` - Shared output formatting (already has code: color.go, size.go, table.go)\n\n\n- `pkg/testutils/` - Differential testing harness\n- `magefiles/` - Build targets (excluded from generation)\n- `docs/` - Specifications (excluded from generation)\n\n**Current State**:\n- Source code exists for: `pkg/format/` (color.go, size.go, table.go)\n- Specs written for many utilities but code generation pending\n- Generated code exists at tags but not on working branch\n\n**Release 00.0** - \"Batch 0 — foundational infrastructure\": Setting up the core build system and testing framework that the rest of the project depends on, with magefiles being excluded from AI generation since they're hand-maintained build configuration.\n\nLooking at the release items, I need to account for what's already implemented versus what still needs work. The format package already has implementations, so that shouldn't be regenerated. The sys and testutils packages still need their code written out from their specs.\n\nGiven the constraint of proposing only one task and the priority that libraries should come before consumers, I'm weighing whether to tackle sys or testutils first. Both are foundational libraries, but I need to trace their dependency relationships to determine which should be implemented first. I can see the PRD naming pattern from the architecture—they follow the format prd[NNN]-<utility>.yaml under docs/specs/product-requirements/. Looking at the analysis section, I've spotted references like prd003-format and prd011-magefiles, so pkg/sys/ would likely be prd002-sys and pkg/testutils/ would be something like prd004-testutils. Since I can't access the actual PRD content without reading files, I should reference these in the required_reading field so the stitch agent can pull them in. Given that pkg/testutils/ is the differential testing harness that all verification depends on, that seems like the most foundational task to propose first. I need to figure out the exact PRD filenames so I can point the stitch agent to them in required_reading, but without being able to read the filesystem directly, I'm working from naming patterns I've inferred from the source code and architecture docs. The stitch agent will need precise file paths to locate the right PRDs, so I should use what I can derive about the numbering scheme and let it search from there. I need to weigh the dependencies more carefully. pkg/sys/ is a prerequisite for pkg/format/, and both are needed by the cmd/ packages, but pkg/testutils/ is the verification harness that validates everything else—making it the most critical single task to propose since the entire project's methodology depends on it for differential testing. I'm seeing that the architecture specifies what pkg/testutils/ should do—execute binaries with identical inputs, compare outputs, and support normalization hooks—but I don't have the exact PRD requirement IDs to anchor to. I'll reference the PRD and use case files generically while deriving the specific requirements from what the architecture describes the component needs to accomplish.\n\nLooking at the naming pattern, I can infer the PRD file likely follows the same structure as the use cases, so I'll reference it in the required reading. For the implementation scope, the core pieces—the DiffTest struct, the runner logic, comparison handling, and normalization support—should fit within the 150-250 line budget, though I'll need to prioritize which requirements to include given the 4-requirement maximum per task. Looking at the PRD numbering pattern more carefully—the architecture references prd011-magefiles in DD7, so the numbering isn't sequential by component. I need to figure out what prd004 actually corresponds to by checking if there's a pattern or if I should look at the existing PRD files to determine the right number for testutils. I need to reference the testutils use case document, the architecture file for component details, and the test suite specs. For the PRD, I'll guess prd004-testutils.yaml but also direct the agent to check the use case file since it should contain the correct PRD reference. Looking at the release structure, I can see the pattern now—rel00.0-uc004-test is the testutils use case in Batch 0.\n\nThe PRD numbering doesn't align sequentially with the use cases (magefiles is prd011, format is prd003), so I can't deduce the testutils PRD number directly. I'll reference the use case and architecture as required reading, then note that the PRD reference should be pulled from the use case itself. The architecture also has specific naming conventions for testutils—the primary file should be difftest.go, not testutils.go, and test files mirror the source structure. I'll focus the task on implementing the core DiffTest type with these constraints in mind. I'm working through the practical constraints here—the stitch agent needs exact file paths to understand the requirements. Looking at the use case naming pattern and the PRD structure, I should reference the testutils use case document directly and note that the agent will need to locate the corresponding PRD file in the product-requirements directory to get the full requirement ID format. I'm overcomplicating this—the stitch agent will read the PRD through required_reading, so my task requirements just need to describe what to build and reference the PRD requirement IDs for alignment. Given the constraints of 4 requirements and 150-250 lines, I can fit the core testutils implementation (DiffTest struct, a runner that executes binaries and compares outputs, and normalization hooks) into a single task, but the principle about splitting implementation and tests into separate tasks suggests I should reconsider the scope.\n\nSince I can only propose one task now, I'll focus on the implementation task and defer the test task to a future batch. Let me draft the task with the proper structure, anchoring to the PRD and architecture docs.\n\nI'm laying out the specific files to create: the DiffTest struct definition, a runner module for executing binaries and capturing their output, and a comparison module that normalizes and validates stdout, stderr, and exit codes. The requirements cover defining the struct with all necessary fields, implementing the dual-binary execution logic, and building the comparison logic.\n\nI'm also noting some key design decisions—the primary file should be named after the exported type per convention, normalization hooks will handle acceptable differences like timestamps, and all tests need to set LC_ALL=C for consistency. The acceptance criteria section is starting to outline what success looks like for this implementation.\n\nNow I'm thinking about how the stitch agent would handle finding the PRD—it can use glob tools to search for the product requirements document in the docs/specs/product-requirements/ directory rather than needing an exact file path.\n\nI'm reconsidering the naming convention based on the existing PRD pattern I've seen, like prd003-format and prd011-magefiles, so for testutils it might be something like prd005-testutils or prd006-testutils depending on the component ordering, though I'm not entirely certain.\n\nLet me restructure the required_reading section to be more practical and actionable, using glob patterns and cross-references to the architecture and use case documents to guide the agent toward the right resources.\n\nActually, the glob pattern might be confusing, so I'm simplifying it to just reference the use case directly, which contains a PRD reference that the agent can follow—that's a more natural workflow than trying to guess the exact filename.\n\nNow I'm thinking about whether the file structure needs adjustment...\n\nwith the 150-250 line target, splitting into just 2 files makes sense: one for the DiffTest struct and public API, another for the execution logic. That keeps concerns separated without over-fragmenting the code. Following the planning constitution, I could structure the tasks as types first, then implementation, then tests.\n\nActually, since the types are small enough to fit within the implementation budget, I'll combine them into a single task. That leaves tests as a separate task, though I can only propose one task here. Let me go with the implementation task that covers both the struct definition and the runner logic. The file count constraint allows up to 5-7 files, so 2-3 files is well within bounds.\n\nNow I'm laying out the task details: I need to create the DiffTest struct with all required fields like input arguments, stdin, environment variables, expected exit code and output, then implement the entry point and binary execution logic with output comparison. The design also includes normalization hooks so callers can register transformation functions to handle acceptable differences like timestamps, and the harness integrates directly with Go's testing framework by accepting *testing.T and reporting failures through t.Errorf. I'm laying out the acceptance criteria: the DiffTest type needs to be exported from difftest.go, a test helper function should run multiple test cases against both binaries, and each case performs byte-for-byte comparison of stdout, stderr, and exit codes.\n\nNow I'm checking the acceptance criteria count against the planning guidelines—I have 5 criteria total, which fits within the 5-8 range for code tasks. The requirements are at 4, which is on the lower end but still acceptable. The design decisions are at 3, which is right in the target range of 3-5.\n\nI'm also verifying that pkg/testutils/ isn't excluded by the architecture constraints—it's in the pkg/ directory which is a valid generation target, so that's clear. The task itself looks self-contained: the stitch agent has everything it needs to read the required files, understand what DiffTest should look like, create the implementation, and verify it compiles. The description and required_reading provide sufficient context for completion. I'm checking the dependency relationships between the candidates—testutils is more foundational since it doesn't depend on sys, whereas sys might rely on other utilities, so testutils should be the priority for this release. I see that pkg/format/ already has a placeholder waiting for pkg/sys/ to handle terminal detection, which means pkg/sys/ is a blocking dependency for cleaner implementations. But testutils is more critical since it's the foundation for the entire verification pipeline—without it, nothing can be tested. I'll go with testutils and make sure to reference the use case ID in the task title since I don't have the exact PRD requirement numbers.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":38461,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4a8555b7-2bf5-4937-bcba-4b409f52fed2","uuid":"8fc04e04-83af-4b78-be78-5f0c6272ee2a"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01WjPaptHTPza7nnc2dhkiCY","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: Classic Unix utilities lack formal specifications. This project derives specs from C source, generates Go implementations via AI (mage-claude-orchestrator), and verifies parity through differential testing against GNU reference binaries.\n\n**Architecture**: Four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify). Core components: `cmd/` (per-utility implementations), `pkg/sys/` (syscall abstractions), `pkg/format/` (output formatting), `pkg/testutils/` (differential testing harness), `magefiles/` (build targets, excluded from generation).\n\n**Current state for release 00.0** (Batch 0 — foundational infrastructure):\n- **rel00.0-uc001-magefiles**: excluded from generation (DD7 — magefiles/ path)\n- **rel00.0-uc002-format**: already implemented (source_code shows color.go, size.go, table.go)\n- **rel00.0-uc003-sys**: spec complete, no code yet\n- **rel00.0-uc004-testutils**: spec complete, no code yet\n\n**Priority reasoning**: `pkg/testutils/` is the differential testing harness — the verification mechanism the entire project depends on. Without it, no utility can be tested against its reference binary. It has no dependencies on `pkg/sys/` (it runs binaries and compares output). Proposing it first unblocks all downstream `cmd/` verification.\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"rel00.0-uc004-testutils: implement DiffTest struct and differential test runner\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml (pkg/testutils/ component section and DiffTest data structure definition)\n      - docs/specs/use-cases/rel00.0-uc004-testutils.yaml (use case -- follow its PRD reference to read the full testutils PRD)\n      - docs/specs/test-suites/test-rel-00.0.yaml (acceptance test specifications for release 00.0)\n\n    files:\n      - path: pkg/testutils/difftest.go\n        action: create\n        note: DiffTest struct, RunDiffTests entry point, binary execution, and output comparison\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Define and export a DiffTest struct with fields for: input arguments, stdin content, environment variables, expected exit code, and expected output -- per the testutils PRD and ARCHITECTURE.yaml DiffTest data structure\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: Implement a function that executes both the Go binary (path provided by caller) and the reference GNU binary (path provided by caller) with identical stdin, arguments, and environment variables\n      - id: R3\n        text: Compare stdout, stderr, and exit code between the two binaries byte-for-byte, with support for caller-provided normalization functions that transform output before comparison (e.g., stripping timestamps)\n      - id: R4\n        text: On divergence, report the input arguments that triggered the mismatch, the reference binary output (expected), and the Go binary output (actual), formatted for go test -v output via t.Errorf\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Primary source file is difftest.go (named after the primary exported type DiffTest), not testutils.go -- per project naming convention P7\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Normalization hooks allow callers to register functions that transform stdout/stderr before comparison, supporting known acceptable differences such as timestamps per ARCHITECTURE.yaml pkg/testutils/ capabilities\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"The harness is designed for use inside go test: callers pass *testing.T, and failures are reported via t.Errorf so they integrate with standard Go test output\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: pkg/testutils/difftest.go compiles and exports the DiffTest type with all specified fields\n      - id: AC2\n        text: A RunDiffTests function accepts a slice of DiffTest cases, a *testing.T, and binary paths, and runs each case against both binaries\n      - id: AC3\n        text: Byte-for-byte comparison of stdout, stderr, and exit code is performed for each test case\n      - id: AC4\n        text: Divergence reports include the triggering input, expected output (reference binary), and actual output (Go binary)\n      - id: AC5\n        text: \"go build ./pkg/testutils/... succeeds with no compilation errors\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":38461,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4a8555b7-2bf5-4937-bcba-4b409f52fed2","uuid":"aada755c-9e9c-4f07-b15d-c737d7df3d6b"}
